"Tackling Hominin Tickling: Bonobos Share the Social Features and Developmental Dynamics of Play Tickling With Humans - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11734381/)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; In humans, a behavior that has been linked to the emergence of intersubjectivity is heavy tickling (here after, tickling), a playful pattern that induces uncontrollable laughter or “gargalesis” (as opposed to “knismesis,” the unpleasant sensation elicited by a light touch across the skin; Hall and Allin 1897; Harris 1999). ... Humans are unable to tickle themselves possibly due to a prediction mechanism that anticipates the sensations deriving from self‐initiated movements and inhibits self‐generated responses (Blakemore, Wolpert, and Frith 2000).\nSuch a framework incites to adopt a bottom‐up perspective and explore if and to what extent intersubjective capacities might be found in species other than humans (de Waal and Ferrari 2010).\n\nIn humans, a behavior that has been linked to the emergence of intersubjectivity is heavy tickling (here after, tickling), a playful pattern that induces uncontrollable laughter or “gargalesis” (as opposed to “knismesis,” the unpleasant sensation elicited by a light touch across the skin; Hall and Allin 1897; Harris 1999). Indeed, the implicit ability to discriminate between self and others' generated stimulation is an essential building block of primary intersubjectivity (Stevanovic and Koski 2018) and a crucial element of gargalesis is that it cannot be self‐induced but requires external triggers (Harris 1999). Humans are unable to tickle themselves possibly due to a prediction mechanism that anticipates the sensations deriving from self‐initiated movements and inhibits self‐generated responses (Blakemore, Wolpert, and Frith 2000). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe neurobiology of ticklishness - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neures.2025.05.002)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ## Neuroscience Research ... Furthermore, loss of knismesis is also observed in patients with functional neurological disorder (Coebergh et al., 2024), a condition linked to altered sense of agency, interoception, and sensory processing (Pareés et al., 2014). ... However, while gargalesis is generally entirely self-inhibitory, knismesis appears less strictly constrained; several authors noted that it can still be self-induced, at least to some extent (Harris, 1999, Varlamov and Skorokhodov, 2022). ... Claxton (1975) proposed that predictability, motor feedback, corollary discharge (i.e. brain’s prediction of sensory outcome), and the absence of a social context contribute to self-tickle inhibition (Claxton, 1975).\n### 3.5. Self-tickling and sensory attenuation\n\n#### 3.5.1. Why can’t we tickle ourselves?\n\nSince Aristotle pondered this phenomenon (Aristotle et al., 1936), the inability to tickle oneself has remained an intriguing mystery. Even when self- and externally generated stimuli are identical (Blakemore et al., 1998, Pridmore et al., 2006, Weiskrantz et al., 1971), and when altered via body transfer illusions (Van Doorn et al., 2014, Van Doorn et al., 2015), self-generated stimulation fails to induce the same knismic sensations. However, while gargalesis is generally entirely self-inhibitory, knismesis appears less strictly constrained; several authors noted that it can still be self-induced, at least to some extent (Harris, 1999, Varlamov and Skorokhodov, 2022). This distinction suggests yet another difference between knismesis and gargalesis in self-generated contexts. Notably, chimpanzees have been observed engaging in self-tickling accompanied by laughter-like vocalizations, suggesting potential exceptions to this rule (Kellogg and Kellogg, 1933, Whiten et al., 2001).\n\n#### 3.5.2. Cerebellar involvement in predicting sensory consequences\n\nNeuroimaging findings confirm sensory attenuation: self-generated knismesis on the palm results in reduced S1 activation compared to externally produced same stimuli, reflecting the reduced perception (Blakemore et al., 1998). This is consistent with broader active vs. passive touch effects in the S1 (Chapman, 1994). Notably, the anterior cerebellum is deactivated during self-generated knismesis, highlighting its role in predicting sensory consequences (Blakemore et al., 1998). When delays or trajectory perturbations, i.e. changes in the orientation of the stimulus, are introduced, perceived knismesis and cerebellar activity progressively increase, demonstrating the brain’s ability to update sensorimotor predictions dynamically (Blakemore et al., 2001, Blakemore et al., 1999b). Cerebellar functional connectivity with S1/S2 correlates with the degree of sensory attenuation upon self-touch (Kilteni and Ehrsson, 2020).\n#### 3.5.4. Altered sense of agency: When self-tickling becomes possible\n\nWhile self-tickling is typically suppressed, disruptions in the sense of agency, the feeling that one is the initiator of their own actions (Haggard, 2017), can override this mechanism. Patients experiencing auditory hallucinations or passivity experiences, where own actions feel externally controlled, report self-induced knismesis as equally intense as externally applied stimuli (Blakemore et al., 2000a). Similar effects occur in individuals with schizotypal traits (Lemaitre et al., 2016, Whitford et al., 2017) and healthy individuals following REM sleep, where sensory attenuation mechanisms are weakened (Blagrove et al., 2006). These findings suggest that predictive coding in sensorimotor control underlies self-tickle suppression, linking it to higher-order cognitive processes regulating agency and voluntary action perception.\n\n### 3.6. Anticipation of ticklish sensations\n\nThe anticipation of tickling engages a complex neural network, preparing the individual for an impending sensory and emotional experience. Notably, regions including the lateral frontal cortices, insula, S1, and S2 exhibit similar activation patterns during both the anticipation and actual experience of knismesis, suggesting that preparatory neural mechanisms overlap with sensory processing.\n\n#### 3.6.2. Why anticipating self- vs. allo-ticklishness produces opposing effects\n\nSeveral factors may contribute to why anticipating external tickling can enhance the sensation, while predicting self-generated tickling suppresses it: i) the absence of an efference copy for externally induced tickling prevents sensory suppression, allowing full perception; ii) heightened attention to an impending ticklish stimulus may amplify sensory salience, increasing perceived intensity; iii) priming in S1 before contact may facilitate early sensory representation, enhancing responsiveness to expected stimulation. While these remain speculative, they suggest that anticipation modulates ticklish responses through predictive and attentional mechanisms.\n\nTogether, anticipation of ticklish sensation is represented in the S1, reflecting early sensory processing before contact. This anticipation is also linked to emotional responses, involving regions such as the LH, NAc, and VTA. Additionally, higher cognitive areas, including the PFC and ACC, contribute to the modulation of ticklish anticipation. The involvement of the ACC and aIC suggests a role for the salience network in detecting and integrating the subjectively significant nature of the anticipated stimulus (Menon and Uddin, 2010), highlighting its integration of sensory, emotional, and cognitive processes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe human tickle response and mechanisms of self-tickle suppression - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9489287/)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; While knismesis is readily elicited on any part of the body, and may be self-evoked, gargalesis is invariably more intricate. ... In-line, self-touch (presumably knismesis) is reliably rated as less ‘tickly’, ‘intense’ or ‘pleasant’ than externally evoked touch sensation [5,6,17]. ... Blue dots show single trial ratings, red lines show the average model prediction, and the shaded area shows the standard error. ... ( 10.6084/m9.figshare.c.6125811) [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed]\nOur study provides new insight on the nature of human ticklishness and the attenuating effects of self-tickling.\n\nThis article is part of the theme issue ‘Cracking the laugh code: laughter through the lens of biology, psychology and neuroscience’.\n\nKeywords: ticklishness, gargalesis, self-tickle suppression, sensory attenuation, self-touch\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nWe might rub and scratch or laugh and wriggle in response to ticklish sensations. In fact, ticklishness describes two very different percepts: one follows touch that is light and feathery, like a spider crawling upon one's skin (termed knismesis), the other touch that is heavy and rhythmic, and commonly associated with playful and interactive behaviour (termed gargalesis; [1]). While knismesis is readily elicited on any part of the body, and may be self-evoked, gargalesis is invariably more intricate. It occurs only in response to touch at certain parts of the body, is dependent on mood and context and generally not evocable through self-touch [2]. It is the only form of touch that provokes laughter, placing it in a unique position of the behavioural repertoire.\n\nEfforts to categorize ticklish responses were undertaken by Hall and Alliń, who conducted a vast survey on ticklishness in children, albeit without considering the distinction of gargalesis and knismesis they went on to coin [1]. They noted that ticklishness varies as a function of body area with the sole of the feet ranking the highest, followed by armpit, neck and chin. Since then, others have identified similar ticklish body sites in adults, if not in the same order and reported without clear methodological description [3,4]. Alas, the nomenclature of ticklishness in human research is frequently used indiscriminately, to describe either knismesis or gargalesis [3]. Arguably, investigations into human tickling often evoke knismesis responses by utilization of equipment such as soft foam or brush to elicit light stimulation (see [5–7]).\n\nGargalesis is not unique to humans but also occurs in other animals including primates [8,9] and rats [10,11]. Rats emit vocalizations associated with positive affect and reward valence during tickling by humans [10,12]. Similar to humans, response to tickling in rats is dependent on body parts and mood [10–13]. Yet, while physiological parameters during tickling have been studied extensively in rats [14], comparative studies in humans are missing.\n\nA remarkable feature of tickle perception is the so-called ‘tickle effect’, referring to the well-known observation that we cannot tickle ourselves [15]. Within somatosensory systems, self-elicited tactile stimulation is perceived with less intensity than the same stimulation applied externally [16]. In-line, self-touch (presumably knismesis) is reliably rated as less ‘tickly’, ‘intense’ or ‘pleasant’ than externally evoked touch sensation [5,6,17]. The prevailing assumption is that lessened perception of a self-tickle is based on the precise predictability of the sensory consequences of self-generated actions [18–22].\n\nIn order to derive a framework of gargalesis and self-touch mediated sensory suppression in humans a physiological characterization is needed. Here, we (i) conducted a time-series analysis of psychophysiological mechanisms (i.e. thoracic circumference, facial expression, vocalizations) during a tickle response and (ii) related these measures to the subjective experience of ticklishness. (iii) Next, we investigated self-touch mediated sensory suppression during coinciding allo- and self-tickling and explored the respective contributions of self-generated motion versus touch in the suppression of ticklishn­ess.\n\n## 4. Discussion\n\nTicklishness in humans and its subjective and physiological features are not well understood. In particular, most research on ticklishness so far has disregarded the distinction between the two types of ticklish sensations, namely knismesis and gargalesis. Here, we performed a systematic assessment of the response pattern characterizing human gargalesis in a controlled environment. We find that (i) the first physiological responses to ticklish touch occur fast after approximately 200 ms, while the vocalization onset occurs substantially later, and that (ii) self-assessment of the intensity of a tickle correlates uniquely to acoustic properties of ticklish laughter. Furthermore, we investigated the effects of self-tickle on ticklishness and find that (iii) self-tickling when co-applied with allo-tickling reduces ticklishness ratings and delays vocalization onset.\n\n### (a) . The timing of the human tickle response\n\nWe show a temporal pattern of a ticklish response with notably faster response latencies than previously reported for tickle stimulation evoking knismesis [7]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe self-reported likelihood of patient delay in breast cancer: new thoughts for early detection - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11914045/)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # The self-reported likelihood of patient delay in breast cancer: new thoughts for early detection ...   * ^{1} University of California San Francisco, California 94143, USA. ... # The self-reported likelihood of patient delay in breast cancer: new thoughts for early detection ...   * Search in PubMed\n\n. 2002 Apr;34(4):397-407.\n\ndoi: 10.1006/pmed.2001.0998.\n\n# The self-reported likelihood of patient delay in breast cancer: new thoughts for early detection\n\nNoreen C Facione^{  1 }, Christine Miaskowski, Marylin J Dodd, Steven M Paul\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} University of California San Francisco, California 94143, USA. Noreen.facione@nursing.ucsf.edu\n\n  * PMID: 11914045\n  * DOI: 10.1006/pmed.2001.0998\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# The self-reported likelihood of patient delay in breast cancer: new thoughts for early detection\n\nNoreen C Facione et al. Prev Med. 2002 Apr.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat [Select]\n\n[Button: Prev Med ]\n\nActions\n\n  * Search in PubMed\n  * Search in NLM Catalog\n  * Add to Search\n\n. 2002 Apr;34(4):397-407.\n\ndoi: 10.1006/pmed.2001.0998.\n\n### Authors\n\nNoreen C Facione^{  1 }, Christine Miaskowski, Marylin J Dodd, Steven M Paul\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} University of California San Francisco, California 94143, USA. Noreen.facione@nursing.ucsf.edu\n\n  * PMID: 11914045\n  * DOI: 10.1006/pmed.2001.0998\n\n## Abstract\n\nBackground: Delayed presentation of self-discovered breast symptoms influences stage of cancer at diagnosis and decreases breast cancer survival.\n\nMethods: A total of 699 asymptomatic women (black, white, and Latino), recruited in community settings and stratified by age, income, and educational level, were surveyed for their likelihood to delay (J-Delay scale) in the event of a breast symptom discovery. Models of likelihood were tested with logistic regression analyses.\n\nResults: A total of 166 women (23.7%) reported likelihood to delay. Lower income, lower educational level, self identification as Latino or black, experienced prejudice in care delivery, perceived lack of access to health care, fatalism about breast cancer, poor health care utilization habits, self-care behavior, spouse/partner and employer perceived constraints, problem-solving style, and a lack of knowledge of breast cancer's presenting symptoms were associated with likelihood to delay. A combined sample multiple logistic regression model correctly predicted 40.6% of women reporting a likelihood to delay, 94.9% of those not likely to delay, and 82.4% (551 of 669) of cases overall.\n\nConclusions: Self-reported likelihood of patient delay is measurable in advance of symptom occurrence, and this measure is consistent with behavioral and knowledge variables previously linked with advanced breast cancer at diagnosis.\n\n(C)2002 Elsevier Science (USA).\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * [Button: Adult ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Breast Neoplasms / diagnosis* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Female ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Health Behavior* / ethnology ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Health Knowledge, Attitudes, Practice ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Health Services Accessibility ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Humans ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Likelihood Functions ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Logistic Models ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScheduling a delay between different surgeons' cases in the same operating room on the same day using upper prediction bounds for case durations - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11273931/)\nciteturn7search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Scheduling a delay between different surgeons' cases in the same operating room on the same day using upper prediction bounds for case durations ...   * Search in PubMed ... Scheduling a delay between the two surgeons' cases will improve the likelihood that the second surgeon's case(s) will start on time.\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Department of Anesthesia, University of Iowa, Iowa City 52242, USA. franklin-dexter@uiowa.edu\n\n  * PMID: 11273931\n  * DOI: 10.1097/00000539-200104000-00028\n\n## Abstract\n\nAt some surgical suites, elective cases are only scheduled if they can be completed during regularly scheduled hours. At such a surgical suite, a surgeon may be scheduled to perform one or more cases in an operating room (OR), to be followed by another surgeon who will perform one or more cases. Scheduling a delay between the two surgeons' cases will improve the likelihood that the second surgeon's case(s) will start on time. We show that the mathematics of calculating a scheduled delay between the different surgeons' cases in the same OR on the same day is that of calculating an upper prediction bound for the duration of the second surgeon's case(s). We test an analytical expression for the upper prediction bound for the last one case of the day in an OR, and a Monte Carlo simulation method for the last two cases. We show that these 90% upper prediction bounds are at least as long as the actual durations for 90% +/- 0.2% of single cases and 92% +/- 0.6% of pairs of cases. We conclude that our methodology can be used to calculate an appropriate, and reasonably accurate, scheduled delay between two surgeons' cases in the same OR on the same day.\n\nImplications: We show how to use a statistical analysis of historical case duration data to calculate an appropriate and accurate scheduled delay between two surgeons' cases in the same operating room on the same day.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * [Button: Algorithms ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Forecasting ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Operating Rooms / organization & administration* ]\n\nActions--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - Leavens_Bard_CurrBio_QG.docx (https://pure.port.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/3431432/Tickling.pdf)\nciteturn7search12 [wordlim: 200] Inside a large rounded blue rectangle: a box labeled “Motor potential” points by a dashed arrow labeled “efferent copy” to a box labeled “Predictor”; from “Predictor” a downward arrow labeled “prediction” goes to a diamond labeled “Self?”. ... Below the diamond are boxes “Knismesis” (left) and “Gargalesis” (right), with arrows up toward “Self?”\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nA 2×2 collage of chimpanzee photos with thin yellow borders: top-left shows a baby chimp sitting on straw with mouth open; top-right shows an adult chimp grooming or holding a baby chimp; bottom-left is a black-and-white photo of a chimp with arms raised while a person reaches toward it; bottom-right shows a small chimp sitting in front of a seated person in blue clothing.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nA schematic diagram about tickle processing and sensory pathways. Inside a large rounded blue rectangle: a box labeled “Motor potential” points by a dashed arrow labeled “efferent copy” to a box labeled “Predictor”; from “Predictor” a downward arrow labeled “prediction” goes to a diamond labeled “Self?”. From “Self?” the left branch marked “Y” leads to a rounded box “NO TICKLE”; the right branch marked “N” leads to a rounded box “TICKLE”. Below the diamond are boxes “Knismesis” (left) and “Gargalesis” (right), with arrows up toward “Self?” and a small label “sensation” beneath the central arrow. Below the blue rectangle are three boxed pathway labels: “Dorsal Column -Fine touch” (orange border), “Lateral Spinothalamic -Pain & Itch” (dark red border), and “Anterior Spinothalamic -Deep touch” (green border). Colored arrows connect these lower boxes upward to the sensation/knismesis/gargalesis area, and a long black arrow descends from “Motor potential” toward the lower left.\n</visual_element>\n\n8--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe J-Delay scale: a measure of the likelihood of patient delay in breast cancer. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12371429/)\nciteturn7search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Journal NLM unique ID: 101146940 ... Patient delay in seeking treatment for breast cancer is a major contributing factor to morbidity and mortality. ...   * Grant ID: F31 NR06851; Acronym: NR; Agency: NINR NIH HHS; Country: United States\nThe J-Delay scale items were developed in four qualitative studies (28 focus groups, combined N = 147 women). The J-Delay scale was tested in 2 large, community-based samples varying across age, income, and ethnicity (combined N = 1,290). Content validity was supported by narrative analysis. Criterion validity was supported by the correct prediction of patient delay in 69%-86% of surveyed and interviewed women with symptoms of 3 months' or more duration. Internal consistency reliability was .83 in English samples (n = 596 and 352) and .81 in a Spanish sample (n = 222). Test-retest reliability after 3-4 months (n = 251) was supported by consistent assignment in 88.8% of cases. The J-Delay is a valid and reliable tool to identify women at risk for patient delay. Women identified as likely to delay medical attention must be targeted for early detection interventions focused on patient delay.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Adult\n  * Anxiety\n  * Breast Neoplasms / psychology\n  * Female\n  * Focus Groups\n  * Health Behavior / ethnology\n  * Health Knowledge, Attitudes, Practice\n  * Humans\n  * Likelihood Functions\n  * Logistic Models\n  * Patient Acceptance of Health Care / ethnology\n  * Patient Acceptance of Health Care / psychology\n  * Time Factors\n\n## Grants\n\n  * Grant ID: F31 NR06851; Acronym: NR; Agency: NINR NIH HHS; Country: United States--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSymptom expectations and delay in acute myocardial infarction patients - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11410572/)\nciteturn7search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Symptom expectations and delay in acute myocardial infarction patients ... # Symptom expectations and delay in acute myocardial infarction patients ...   * Search in PubMed  * Search in NLM Catalog\n\n### Full text links\n\nHighWire Free PMC article\n\nFull text links\n\n. 2001 Jul;86(1):91-3.\n\ndoi: 10.1136/heart.86.1.91.\n\n# Symptom expectations and delay in acute myocardial infarction patients\n\nK Perry, K J Petrie, C J Ellis, R Horne, R Moss-Morris\n\n  * PMID: 11410572\n  * PMCID: PMC1729795\n  * DOI: 10.1136/heart.86.1.91\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Symptom expectations and delay in acute myocardial infarction patients\n\nK Perry et al. Heart. 2001 Jul.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat [Select]\n\n[Button: Heart ]\n\nActions\n\n  * Search in PubMed\n  * Search in NLM Catalog\n  * Add to Search\n\n. 2001 Jul;86(1):91-3.\n\ndoi: 10.1136/heart.86.1.91.\n\n### Authors\n\nK Perry, K J Petrie, C J Ellis, R Horne, R Moss-Morris\n\n  * PMID: 11410572\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Communication ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Female ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Humans ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Male ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Middle Aged ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Myocardial Infarction / drug therapy ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Myocardial Infarction / psychology* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Patient Acceptance of Health Care* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Self Medication ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Thrombolytic Therapy / methods ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Time Factors ]\n\nActions\n\n\n## LinkOut - more resources\n\n  * ### Full Text Sources\n\n    * Europe PMC\n    * HighWire\n    * Ovid Technologies, Inc.\n    * PubMed Central--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy You Can’t Tickle Yourself | Quirk Lab (https://quirklabscience.com/expressive-quirks/why-you-cant-tickle-yourself)\nciteturn7search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The prediction is the whole mechanism, and it explains not only why self-tickling fails but exactly what has to change for it to succeed. ... Knismesis is the light, itch-like tickle from a feather or insect, and can be self-produced.\n\n# Why You Can’t Tickle Yourself\n\nYour brain cancels your own touch before you feel it. That’s why self-tickling fails.\n\nGiorgi Chubinidze·5 min read·ExpressionChecked against primary sources\n\nImage: A feather resting across an open upturned palm.\n\nOn this page\n\n  * Two tickles, one word\n  * The forward model\n  * The experiment that breaks the prediction\n  * Where else this machinery shows up\n  * Is tickle-laughter even about humour?\n  * Why the approach is worse than the touch\n  * What remains unexplained\n  * Frequently asked questions\n\nTwo different sensations share one word. Knismesis is the light, itch-like tickle, and you can produce it on yourself. Gargalesis is the heavy, laughter-inducing kind, and you cannot — because your cerebellum uses a copy of your own motor commands to predict the sensory consequences of your movements and cancels them out in advance. Introduce a delay of a few hundred milliseconds between your action and the touch, and self-tickling starts to work.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy You Cannot Tickle Yourself — DidYeah (https://didyeah.com/facts/you-cannot-tickle-yourself-but-your-brain-predicts-the-touch)\nciteturn7search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Even then, the hand doing the moving supplies extra feedback that helps the prediction. ... The claim refers mainly to laughter-provoking gargalesis; light itchy knismesis can be partly self-evoked. ... DidYeah Editorial·published 24 August 2026·checked against The extraordinary enigma of ordinary tickle behavior: Why gargalesis still puzzles neuroscience – Royal Society and Why can't you tickle yourself? – BMJ Publishing Group·how we check facts\nThe somatosensory cortex, which maps touch from the body, then receives a less startling version of the event.\n\nThis is called reafference, the sensory feedback produced by your own action. It is useful for separating a finger brushing your arm from somebody else's finger doing it. In self-tickle experiments, people rated externally produced touch as stronger and ticklier than matching self-produced touch. Brain imaging also found less activity in primary somatosensory areas during self-generated contact.\n\nThe rule is about the laughter-provoking form of tickling, called gargalesis, rather than every itchy or feather-light sensation. A person can sometimes create a weak knismesis response, the light crawling or prickling kind, on their own skin. Even then, the hand doing the moving supplies extra feedback that helps the prediction.\n\nA robot can make the trick feel different by moving the tickling tool after your command instead of at the expected instant. The moment the contact slips out of sync, your own hand starts behaving like an outside event, and the skin gets its surprise back.\n\n## Worth being precise about\n\nThe claim refers mainly to laughter-provoking gargalesis; light itchy knismesis can be partly self-evoked.\n\nDid it stick?\n\n## What helps make a self-tickle feel weak?\n\n  1. A A prediction of the touch\n  2. B Extra skin receptors\n  3. C A lower body temperature\n\nAnswer: A — A prediction of the touch\n\nDidYeah Editorial·published 24 August 2026·checked against The extraordinary enigma of ordinary tickle behavior: Why gargalesis still puzzles neuroscience – Royal Society and Why can't you tickle yourself? – BMJ Publishing Group·how we check facts--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy do cancer patients delay? (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13276881/)\nciteturn7search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 70.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Why do cancer patients delay? ... Journal NLM unique ID: 0414110\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKnismesis vs Gargalesis: The Two Kinds of Tickle (https://ticklewell.com/knismesis-vs-gargalesis/)\nciteturn7search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Knismesis vs Gargalesis: The Two Kinds of Tickle ... Knismesis is the light one. ... When you reach for your own ribs, that prediction arrives fractionally before the touch, and the brain attenuates the sensation. ... Brain imaging work on self-generated touch has found reduced cerebellar activity when a movement produces a tactile stimulus compared with when it does not, which is what the model predicts.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelay in calling for help during myocardial infarction: reasons for the delay and subsequent pattern of accessing care - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1760908/)\nciteturn7search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 26.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Keywords: myocardial infarction; treatment delay ... [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n------